WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.508 --> 00:00:03.925
- You look for that thing that moves you.

00:00:05.629 --> 00:00:08.340
At that point, I thought this is what I do

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.176
with my life, and nothing mattered,

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:13.499
being famous, being rich, nothing.

00:00:13.499 --> 00:00:14.765
Only doing this.

00:00:14.765 --> 00:00:17.598
Desire can sometimes trump talent.

00:00:18.514 --> 00:00:21.728
- He's an American actor,
filmmaker, and screenwriter.

00:00:21.728 --> 00:00:24.070
He's had an acting
career spanning 50 years,

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:25.701
and along that way has received

00:00:25.701 --> 00:00:27.381
numerous awards and accolades.

00:00:27.381 --> 00:00:28.629
His performance as Michael Corleone

00:00:28.629 --> 00:00:30.537
in The Godfather is widely regarded

00:00:30.537 --> 00:00:33.847
as one of the greatest screen
performances in film history.

00:00:33.847 --> 00:00:35.638
He's Al Pacino, and here's my take

00:00:35.638 --> 00:00:37.435
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:37.435 --> 00:00:38.746
Rule number two is my personal favorite,

00:00:38.746 --> 00:00:40.405
and make sure to stick around all the way

00:00:40.405 --> 00:00:42.210
to the end for some special bonus clips.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:43.613
Also, as Al is talking,
if he says something

00:00:43.613 --> 00:00:45.173
really meaningful to you, please

00:00:45.173 --> 00:00:46.940
leave it in the comments below

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:51.107
and put quotes around it, so
others can be inspired as well.

00:00:52.545 --> 00:00:55.962
- You look for that thing that moves you,

00:00:57.762 --> 00:01:01.193
I don't know what it
is, I like repetition.

00:01:01.193 --> 00:01:04.170
And as the saying goes, repetition,

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:06.452
repetition keeps me green.

00:01:06.452 --> 00:01:07.933
I love that saying.

00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:09.544
- [Interviewer] Repetition keeps me green.

00:01:09.544 --> 00:01:13.267
- Keeps me green, fresh because the idea

00:01:13.267 --> 00:01:16.432
that we do performances
over, and over again

00:01:16.432 --> 00:01:18.929
you'd say well doesn't
that get boring or stale,

00:01:18.929 --> 00:01:21.270
but no it's in repetition that

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.521
the creation comes, that the
expression comes because,

00:01:24.521 --> 00:01:26.886
I'll give you an example,
I was doing Richard

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:29.370
once and not particularly well,

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.600
and this show is going on, and we couldn't

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.480
get this court scene with
a lot of people in it.

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:37.589
I didn't understand what
I was doing in the scene.

00:01:37.589 --> 00:01:39.123
So, we would call rehearsals and I

00:01:39.123 --> 00:01:41.333
would, we would rehearse it and stuff,

00:01:41.333 --> 00:01:43.637
and we would talk about it, think about it

00:01:43.637 --> 00:01:47.033
you know like all this kind of work

00:01:47.033 --> 00:01:49.208
where people together are
trying to figure out something.

00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:51.875
The 85th performance of Richard,

00:01:53.261 --> 00:01:57.428
I had done it, my 85th entrance,
I knew the court scene.

00:01:59.003 --> 00:02:02.393
I understood it, I was
there, I could play in it.

00:02:02.393 --> 00:02:03.614
- [Interviewer] But not in 84?

00:02:03.614 --> 00:02:04.790
- Not in 84!

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:07.010
I might've been getting
closer than realized,

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:09.049
I gave up the ghost, I guess.

00:02:09.049 --> 00:02:10.589
I just gave it up and said oh, screw it,

00:02:10.589 --> 00:02:11.938
it's not going to happen and

00:02:11.938 --> 00:02:13.514
I'll just keep going on, and I found it.

00:02:13.514 --> 00:02:17.058
All train compartment smell vaguely of,

00:02:17.058 --> 00:02:19.475
I guess so you don't mind it.

00:02:20.896 --> 00:02:23.961
That's the worse thing that I can confess,

00:02:23.961 --> 00:02:25.602
you know how long it took me to get there?

00:02:25.602 --> 00:02:26.602
A long time.

00:02:28.316 --> 00:02:32.045
When you die, you're going to
regret the things you don't do.

00:02:32.045 --> 00:02:34.074
You think you're queer, I'm going to

00:02:34.074 --> 00:02:35.929
tell you something, we're all queer.

00:02:35.929 --> 00:02:38.216
You think you're a thief, so what?

00:02:38.216 --> 00:02:40.675
You get befuddled by
middle class morality,

00:02:40.675 --> 00:02:42.518
get shut of it, shut it out.

00:02:42.518 --> 00:02:46.268
Cheat on your wife, you
did it, live with it.

00:02:47.316 --> 00:02:49.566
You little girls, so be it.

00:02:50.767 --> 00:02:52.115
There's an absolute morality?

00:02:52.115 --> 00:02:52.948
Maybe.

00:02:53.875 --> 00:02:55.042
And then what?

00:02:56.685 --> 00:02:59.409
If you think there is,
go ahead be that thing.

00:02:59.409 --> 00:03:01.721
Bad people go to hell?

00:03:01.721 --> 00:03:02.887
I don't think so.

00:03:02.887 --> 00:03:03.792
You think that?

00:03:03.792 --> 00:03:05.517
Act that way.

00:03:05.517 --> 00:03:07.099
Hell exists on earth?

00:03:07.099 --> 00:03:07.932
Yes.

00:03:09.406 --> 00:03:10.733
I won't live in it.

00:03:10.733 --> 00:03:14.068
I would like to be
someone who takes chances,

00:03:14.068 --> 00:03:16.521
I think part of the adventure of it

00:03:16.521 --> 00:03:20.688
is to go into something, and
not be afraid to screw up.

00:03:22.113 --> 00:03:23.888
Because then you're
reaching for something.

00:03:23.888 --> 00:03:24.721
Come on!

00:03:26.288 --> 00:03:28.826
- Why are you doing this, Roy?

00:03:28.826 --> 00:03:30.483
- No, I mean it, say it.

00:03:30.483 --> 00:03:33.400
Say Roy Cohn, you are a homosexual.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:38.717
And I will proceed, systematically,

00:03:39.791 --> 00:03:43.624
to destroy your reputation,
and your practice,

00:03:44.973 --> 00:03:45.865
and your career.

00:03:45.865 --> 00:03:48.685
I think what happens is you
learn to forgive yourself,

00:03:48.685 --> 00:03:51.516
because you see in the scheme of things

00:03:51.516 --> 00:03:54.482
how it is, and you say well
gee, you know, I tried.

00:03:54.482 --> 00:03:58.726
The worst thing, as they
say, is not to go for it.

00:03:58.726 --> 00:04:00.700
But, if you go for it and it goes,

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:03.147
you know, it's out of your hands

00:04:03.147 --> 00:04:06.564
and it's like that old saying what anyone

00:04:09.217 --> 00:04:13.694
ever says about me is
really none of my business.

00:04:13.694 --> 00:04:15.362
So, you know, you sort of you go

00:04:15.362 --> 00:04:18.950
and you hope for the
best, and then you see

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:21.409
that a lot of the times, you know,

00:04:21.409 --> 00:04:25.242
things happen and you
have no control over it.

00:04:26.545 --> 00:04:30.045
Alright, let's get a message to Joey Zasa.

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:35.810
I respect what he's done, the new

00:04:36.856 --> 00:04:40.392
overthrows the old, it's natural.

00:04:40.392 --> 00:04:44.338
- How could you do business with this guy?

00:04:44.338 --> 00:04:48.095
- I'm a business man, first foremost,

00:04:48.095 --> 00:04:49.800
I want no further conflict for that.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:51.032
- You can tell him for me that he

00:04:51.032 --> 00:04:52.154
could live, or he could die.

00:04:52.154 --> 00:04:54.487
- Vincent, will you shut up!

00:04:57.648 --> 00:05:00.231
(loud thunder)

00:05:11.963 --> 00:05:12.796
That's it.

00:05:16.283 --> 00:05:17.116
Joey Zasa.

00:05:18.539 --> 00:05:20.956
He can't be doing this alone.

00:05:24.599 --> 00:05:29.434
Just when I thought I was
out, they pull me back in.

00:05:29.434 --> 00:05:31.355
If you're going to make a movie, right,

00:05:31.355 --> 00:05:35.323
the whole idea is that you make a movie

00:05:35.323 --> 00:05:38.189
about something, and to be able

00:05:38.189 --> 00:05:40.052
to make a movie about something you know

00:05:40.052 --> 00:05:43.688
something that's sort of in
your wheelhouse, so to speak.

00:05:43.688 --> 00:05:45.163
It's something that you relate to.

00:05:45.163 --> 00:05:49.412
It's important because
making a movie is so hard,

00:05:49.412 --> 00:05:54.129
it's very difficult to get a
movie together and, oh man.

00:05:54.129 --> 00:05:56.761
You know, you've heard it many times.

00:05:56.761 --> 00:05:59.757
But to have something
you feel you understand,

00:05:59.757 --> 00:06:02.264
that's why I had a lot of
fun doing a movie I made

00:06:02.264 --> 00:06:03.797
called Looking for Richard.

00:06:03.797 --> 00:06:06.957
It was sort of about actors trying

00:06:06.957 --> 00:06:09.212
to figure out how to do Shakespeare,

00:06:09.212 --> 00:06:10.927
then people and everything else.

00:06:10.927 --> 00:06:13.723
So it had a very visceral
connection to it.

00:06:13.723 --> 00:06:17.537
Well, a movie, a book about an actor

00:06:17.537 --> 00:06:21.978
going downhill and losing
it, and getting older,

00:06:21.978 --> 00:06:25.833
and falling apart seemed very
appropriate for me, you know?

00:06:25.833 --> 00:06:29.249
And I thought this is going to work.

00:06:29.249 --> 00:06:31.832
The one thing that bothered me,

00:06:33.165 --> 00:06:36.158
the one thing that stayed in my mind,

00:06:36.158 --> 00:06:40.325
and I couldn't get rid of
it, that haunted me, was why.

00:06:43.995 --> 00:06:45.495
Why would she lie?

00:06:48.786 --> 00:06:51.309
What was her motive for lying?

00:06:51.309 --> 00:06:54.476
If my client is innocent, she's lying.

00:06:56.122 --> 00:06:56.955
Why?

00:06:59.495 --> 00:07:00.912
Was it blackmail?

00:07:02.217 --> 00:07:03.050
No.

00:07:05.013 --> 00:07:06.346
Was it jealousy?

00:07:08.023 --> 00:07:08.856
No.

00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:13.660
Yesterday, I found out why.

00:07:21.824 --> 00:07:23.991
She doesn't have a motive.

00:07:26.230 --> 00:07:27.313
You know why?

00:07:31.364 --> 00:07:33.364
Because she's not lying.

00:07:35.669 --> 00:07:38.673
And, ladies and gentleman of the jury,

00:07:38.673 --> 00:07:42.840
the prosecution is not going
to get that man today, no.

00:07:48.381 --> 00:07:50.548
Because I'm going to get him.

00:07:51.595 --> 00:07:53.901
Everything I do, every day, every day,

00:07:53.901 --> 00:07:56.989
whether they're working on
the play, the whole thing,

00:07:56.989 --> 00:07:59.439
everything has to get to a point

00:07:59.439 --> 00:08:01.772
where you don't have act it.

00:08:03.291 --> 00:08:07.011
That all you're doing is
going through the thing,

00:08:07.011 --> 00:08:09.094
and you're not acting it.

00:08:10.041 --> 00:08:12.322
You just know what you're
talking about so much

00:08:12.322 --> 00:08:15.481
that you don't have to act it.

00:08:15.481 --> 00:08:16.815
If you know what you're saying,

00:08:16.815 --> 00:08:20.101
you know why you're
saying it, and what you're

00:08:20.101 --> 00:08:23.282
talking about, and who
you're talking about,

00:08:23.282 --> 00:08:25.546
and how you feel when you talk about it,

00:08:25.546 --> 00:08:27.348
then you don't have to act it.

00:08:27.348 --> 00:08:29.711
And that's what the whole thing's about.

00:08:29.711 --> 00:08:31.919
Alright, alright, that's enough.

00:08:31.919 --> 00:08:34.115
I want 'em dead, both of them.

00:08:34.115 --> 00:08:36.981
I want this no-face dead,
and I want Tracy dead.

00:08:36.981 --> 00:08:37.954
What's a matter?

00:08:37.954 --> 00:08:39.781
You bums forgot how to kill people?

00:08:39.781 --> 00:08:41.759
Doesn't your work mean
anything to you anymore?

00:08:41.759 --> 00:08:44.580
Have you no sense of pride in what you do?

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:46.609
No sense of duty?

00:08:46.609 --> 00:08:48.310
No sense of destiny?

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:52.861
I'm looking for generals,
why do I got foot soldiers?

00:08:52.861 --> 00:08:54.778
I want Dick Tracy dead!

00:08:56.373 --> 00:08:58.759
Yeah, I often say to people who talk to me

00:08:58.759 --> 00:09:01.615
about Shakespeare, who are not actors

00:09:01.615 --> 00:09:04.739
or directors, who haven't
ever played it or done it.

00:09:04.739 --> 00:09:09.008
The actor learns
Shakespeare in another way,

00:09:09.008 --> 00:09:11.557
because he learns it having to do it,

00:09:11.557 --> 00:09:13.387
having to spend months with it.

00:09:13.387 --> 00:09:15.222
So, he approaches it in that way

00:09:15.222 --> 00:09:17.158
and understands it in that way,

00:09:17.158 --> 00:09:19.004
and the familiarity with the words

00:09:19.004 --> 00:09:22.765
and the phrases, and what the content is,

00:09:22.765 --> 00:09:25.679
what it's saying becomes a part of you.

00:09:25.679 --> 00:09:27.709
And the revelations keep coming

00:09:27.709 --> 00:09:31.148
long after you've closed in a play.

00:09:31.148 --> 00:09:34.114
You know, you know things,
and you hear things

00:09:34.114 --> 00:09:37.222
in a way that you could
never if you saw the play,

00:09:37.222 --> 00:09:39.428
or if you read the play, no.

00:09:39.428 --> 00:09:41.675
That's why actors, in a way, are lucky

00:09:41.675 --> 00:09:43.721
that they get an opportunity to,

00:09:43.721 --> 00:09:45.019
that's why some of the great ones

00:09:45.019 --> 00:09:48.682
that had the repertoires
of plays they did,

00:09:48.682 --> 00:09:53.491
so wanted to do it because
it had so much in it.

00:09:53.491 --> 00:09:56.074
It's almost like, I can come close to say,

00:09:56.074 --> 00:09:59.376
say you were a cello, you
played the cello or something,

00:09:59.376 --> 00:10:01.907
and you were playing Bach on the cello.

00:10:01.907 --> 00:10:04.511
I mean, wow, imagine that.

00:10:04.511 --> 00:10:07.762
You know, so that manifestation

00:10:07.762 --> 00:10:10.162
of spirit which comes out of it

00:10:10.162 --> 00:10:13.797
changes you, it lifts your life, it does.

00:10:13.797 --> 00:10:15.851
It gives you so much.

00:10:15.851 --> 00:10:18.635
And once that appeal to me, when I

00:10:18.635 --> 00:10:22.330
was very young actually,
I was a very young guy

00:10:22.330 --> 00:10:26.016
when I started to do Strindberg
and things like that,

00:10:26.016 --> 00:10:28.071
and was very affected
by that, cause I thought

00:10:28.071 --> 00:10:32.833
here it is with this material,
it's like playing the cello

00:10:32.833 --> 00:10:36.445
because I can kind of just express things

00:10:36.445 --> 00:10:39.495
in myself, and it made
me sort of universal,

00:10:39.495 --> 00:10:42.926
it gave me a more universal sense

00:10:42.926 --> 00:10:46.000
of things and myself,
because I was doing things

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:48.757
that happened years and years ago.

00:10:48.757 --> 00:10:50.902
I was out of the south Bronx.

00:10:50.902 --> 00:10:53.653
I was out of the life I come from,

00:10:53.653 --> 00:10:56.832
into another world that was
giving me all this stuff.

00:10:56.832 --> 00:10:58.330
It was a gift.

00:10:58.330 --> 00:11:01.045
And I knew from that point on, age 21,

00:11:01.045 --> 00:11:04.693
I knew at that point I had been
acting since I was this big,

00:11:04.693 --> 00:11:08.927
but at that point I though
this is what I do with my life.

00:11:08.927 --> 00:11:13.559
And nothing mattered, being
famous, being rich, nothing.

00:11:13.559 --> 00:11:14.761
Only doing this.

00:11:14.761 --> 00:11:17.016
Now at 21, that's easy
to say you know, I mean.

00:11:17.016 --> 00:11:19.881
There's just you and
pizza, so you're fine.

00:11:19.881 --> 00:11:21.787
(laughter)

00:11:21.787 --> 00:11:24.051
But, it is true though,
that did happen to me.

00:11:24.051 --> 00:11:26.019
How many Ferraris you
sold this month, though?

00:11:26.019 --> 00:11:27.952
- It's not relevant to this discussion.

00:11:27.952 --> 00:11:29.946
- Freddie, the 80s are over.

00:11:29.946 --> 00:11:32.358
Are you trying to tell me these things

00:11:32.358 --> 00:11:34.195
are just walking out of the store?

00:11:34.195 --> 00:11:36.361
- This is a Ferrari sir,
this is the finest piece

00:11:36.361 --> 00:11:38.526
of machinery made in
the automobile industry.

00:11:38.526 --> 00:11:39.597
- Well, if you like it that much,

00:11:39.597 --> 00:11:40.624
why don't you sleep with it?

00:11:40.624 --> 00:11:41.730
Why are you selling it?

00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:43.123
- Listen, I'd love to accommodate you.

00:11:43.123 --> 00:11:44.671
- If this car performs the way

00:11:44.671 --> 00:11:47.346
I expect it to, you will
get a certified check

00:11:47.346 --> 00:11:50.672
of 101,000 and change when you come

00:11:50.672 --> 00:11:52.002
in here tomorrow morning.

00:11:52.002 --> 00:11:55.923
- It's 109,000 plus 950 dollars, plus tax.

00:11:55.923 --> 00:11:58.506
- Freddie, for you, 107 all in,

00:12:00.991 --> 00:12:05.581
plus a case of champagne, go
with your leftover turkey.

00:12:05.581 --> 00:12:07.192
What do you say?

00:12:07.192 --> 00:12:09.901
Don't worry about the
boy, he drives so smooth

00:12:09.901 --> 00:12:11.739
you can boil an egg on the engine.

00:12:11.739 --> 00:12:15.654
When we bring the car back,
I'll peel the egg for ya.

00:12:15.654 --> 00:12:18.487
Desire can sometimes trump talent.

00:12:21.783 --> 00:12:23.200
Think about that.

00:12:24.539 --> 00:12:27.086
So that you may not have as much talent

00:12:27.086 --> 00:12:29.836
as you think you have, but if you

00:12:31.441 --> 00:12:35.832
have the desire, your
talent will find you.

00:12:35.832 --> 00:12:38.415
I never anybody over in my life

00:12:39.708 --> 00:12:41.234
didn't have it comin' to him.

00:12:41.234 --> 00:12:42.484
You got that?

00:12:42.484 --> 00:12:45.307
All I have in this world is my balls

00:12:45.307 --> 00:12:48.676
and my word, and I don't
break 'em for no one.

00:12:48.676 --> 00:12:49.926
You understand?

00:12:51.604 --> 00:12:53.537
That piece of up there, I never like him,

00:12:53.537 --> 00:12:55.261
I never trusted him.

00:12:55.261 --> 00:12:57.322
For all I know, he had me set up

00:12:57.322 --> 00:12:59.423
and had my friend Angel Fernandez killed,

00:12:59.423 --> 00:13:01.449
but that's history.

00:13:01.449 --> 00:13:02.841
I'm here, he's not.

00:13:02.841 --> 00:13:04.256
You want to go on with me, you say it.

00:13:04.256 --> 00:13:07.173
You don't, then you make a move.

00:13:07.173 --> 00:13:09.533
Cause I'm making the films myself,

00:13:09.533 --> 00:13:11.383
nobody sees them of course.

00:13:11.383 --> 00:13:13.338
But, I make them on my own.

00:13:13.338 --> 00:13:15.296
I made them, in the old days when I

00:13:15.296 --> 00:13:17.430
had it, I used to make
them with my own money.

00:13:17.430 --> 00:13:19.953
And that was kind of
strange, I was accused

00:13:19.953 --> 00:13:22.453
of being crazy for doing that.

00:13:23.403 --> 00:13:24.320
And, I was.

00:13:25.580 --> 00:13:28.663
But it was important for
me to learn film that way,

00:13:28.663 --> 00:13:31.496
so I like it, I like to mix it up.

00:13:32.830 --> 00:13:34.321
- That plane goes to Miami.

00:13:34.321 --> 00:13:35.682
- Okay.

00:13:35.682 --> 00:13:38.242
That's where I want it met.

00:13:38.242 --> 00:13:40.162
- Mike, that's impossible.

00:13:40.162 --> 00:13:41.324
They'll tune him over directly

00:13:41.324 --> 00:13:43.806
to the Internal Revenue
Customs and half the FBI.

00:13:43.806 --> 00:13:45.936
- It's not impossible.

00:13:45.936 --> 00:13:47.506
Nothing's impossible.

00:13:47.506 --> 00:13:48.959
- It would be like trying
to kill the president,

00:13:48.959 --> 00:13:50.372
there's no way we can get to him.

00:13:50.372 --> 00:13:53.122
- Tom, you know, you surprise me.

00:13:55.834 --> 00:13:58.834
If anything in this life is certain,

00:13:59.916 --> 00:14:03.066
if history's taught us anything,

00:14:03.066 --> 00:14:04.886
it's that you can kill anyone.

00:14:04.886 --> 00:14:08.677
I suggest reading, of course it's tough

00:14:08.677 --> 00:14:10.636
reading books these days, but you should.

00:14:10.636 --> 00:14:12.878
I really believe in it.

00:14:12.878 --> 00:14:16.395
Actually cause, you know, the Russians

00:14:16.395 --> 00:14:20.750
saved my life when I was
very young, like your age.

00:14:20.750 --> 00:14:24.500
And doing plays, if you
can if you're actors,

00:14:25.538 --> 00:14:28.705
because the plays are what, roles that

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:33.487
are challenge you and stuff
are really good for you.

00:14:33.487 --> 00:14:35.387
Because also, you're
doing plays sometimes,

00:14:35.387 --> 00:14:37.970
that ask of you a lot of stuff.

00:14:38.823 --> 00:14:41.787
I mean it asks you to do more than some

00:14:41.787 --> 00:14:43.346
of these roles we get cast for

00:14:43.346 --> 00:14:45.697
that don't ask that much of us, really.

00:14:45.697 --> 00:14:47.978
I mean if naturalism, we do it you know,

00:14:47.978 --> 00:14:51.313
you do this, but the roles that ask a lot

00:14:51.313 --> 00:14:54.794
of you is where you develop, where you

00:14:54.794 --> 00:14:58.127
broaden your horizons, your imagination.

00:15:00.517 --> 00:15:02.918
And what you learn from
this great literature,

00:15:02.918 --> 00:15:04.730
I mean if you're a cellist right,

00:15:04.730 --> 00:15:07.708
you're going to want to play Bach, no?

00:15:07.708 --> 00:15:09.277
I mean, why wouldn't you?

00:15:09.277 --> 00:15:12.691
You're a cellist, Bach wrote for you.

00:15:12.691 --> 00:15:14.811
And eventually you want to play it,

00:15:14.811 --> 00:15:16.505
you play anything on it, but finally

00:15:16.505 --> 00:15:18.246
that's what you want to play because

00:15:18.246 --> 00:15:21.425
that's going to get you the kind of joy,

00:15:21.425 --> 00:15:23.675
the kind of excitement that

00:15:25.025 --> 00:15:26.981
is going to change your life, really.

00:15:26.981 --> 00:15:29.202
Changed my life, a lot
of the parts I played

00:15:29.202 --> 00:15:32.452
as a youngster made me broaden my scope

00:15:35.741 --> 00:15:37.961
and my understanding of things.

00:15:37.961 --> 00:15:42.128
It's really important to
open yourself up to that.

00:15:43.169 --> 00:15:44.586
City cop, right?

00:15:44.586 --> 00:15:46.071
Robbing a bank's a federal offense.

00:15:46.071 --> 00:15:47.882
They got me on kidnapping, armed robbery,

00:15:47.882 --> 00:15:49.350
they're going to bury me, man.

00:15:49.350 --> 00:15:51.774
I don't want to talk to
somebody who's trying to con me,

00:15:51.774 --> 00:15:53.009
get somebody in charge here.

00:15:53.009 --> 00:15:53.922
- I am in charge here.

00:15:53.922 --> 00:15:55.335
- I don't want to talk to some flunky pig

00:15:55.335 --> 00:15:57.083
trying to con me, what's he doing?

00:15:57.083 --> 00:15:58.923
- Will you get back over there?

00:15:58.923 --> 00:16:00.316
- What are they moving in there for?

00:16:00.316 --> 00:16:01.517
- Will you get the fuck back there?

00:16:01.517 --> 00:16:02.551
Get back there, what are you doing?

00:16:02.551 --> 00:16:03.726
Hey, get over there!

00:16:03.726 --> 00:16:05.079
- Go on back there, man.

00:16:05.079 --> 00:16:06.522
- [Cop] Get over there, will ya?

00:16:06.522 --> 00:16:08.667
- He wants to kill me
so bad he can taste it.

00:16:08.667 --> 00:16:09.999
- [Cop] Nobody's going to kill anybody.

00:16:09.999 --> 00:16:10.832
- Attica!

00:16:10.832 --> 00:16:12.017
Attica!

00:16:12.017 --> 00:16:13.517
My instincts, I always felt, were better

00:16:13.517 --> 00:16:16.902
earlier in my life than they are now,

00:16:16.902 --> 00:16:18.384
it seems as though sometimes I'm a

00:16:18.384 --> 00:16:20.273
little bit blinded by the the attention

00:16:20.273 --> 00:16:23.234
so I don't make correct assessments.

00:16:23.234 --> 00:16:25.887
I'm a little bit influenced by the fact

00:16:25.887 --> 00:16:29.137
that I'm not on the outside looking in.

00:16:29.137 --> 00:16:32.270
I was fortunate enough
to have certain things

00:16:32.270 --> 00:16:34.184
happen in my life, I mean it's luck.

00:16:34.184 --> 00:16:36.068
It's really luck.

00:16:36.068 --> 00:16:39.566
And persisting in a thing, you know.

00:16:39.566 --> 00:16:43.648
What is that, he who persists in his folly

00:16:43.648 --> 00:16:47.148
will one day be wise, and I'm not wise yet

00:16:48.050 --> 00:16:49.739
but I keep persisting.

00:16:49.739 --> 00:16:52.489
(dramatic music)

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:03.458
Mr. Simms doesn't want it.

00:17:03.458 --> 00:17:06.518
He doesn't need to be labeled still worthy

00:17:06.518 --> 00:17:08.330
of being a Baird man.

00:17:08.330 --> 00:17:09.827
What the hell is that?

00:17:09.827 --> 00:17:12.144
What is your motto here?

00:17:12.144 --> 00:17:16.781
Boys, inform on your
classmates, save your hide.

00:17:16.781 --> 00:17:18.098
Anything short of that, we're going to

00:17:18.098 --> 00:17:19.824
burn you at the stake?

00:17:19.824 --> 00:17:23.820
Well gentleman, when the shit hits the fan

00:17:23.820 --> 00:17:26.653
some guys run, and some guys stay.

00:17:27.746 --> 00:17:29.906
Here's Charlie facing the fire

00:17:29.906 --> 00:17:33.447
and there's George hiding
in big daddy's pocket.

00:17:33.447 --> 00:17:35.018
And what are you doing?

00:17:35.018 --> 00:17:39.018
You're going to reward
George, and destroy Charlie.

00:17:41.119 --> 00:17:42.821
- Are you finished Mr. Slade?

00:17:42.821 --> 00:17:45.120
- No, I'm just getting warmed up.

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:47.941
I don't know who went to this place,

00:17:47.941 --> 00:17:50.797
William Howard Taft,
William Jennings Bryant,

00:17:50.797 --> 00:17:52.483
William Tell, whoever.

00:17:52.483 --> 00:17:57.015
Their spirit is dead if they
ever had one, it's gone.

00:17:57.015 --> 00:17:59.453
You're building a rat ship here,

00:17:59.453 --> 00:18:02.036
a vessel for seagoing snitches,

00:18:03.668 --> 00:18:04.924
and if you think you're preparing

00:18:04.924 --> 00:18:08.790
these minnows for manhood,
you better think again.

00:18:08.790 --> 00:18:11.129
Because I say you are
killing the very spirit

00:18:11.129 --> 00:18:15.111
this institution proclaims it instills.

00:18:15.111 --> 00:18:16.111
What a sham.

00:18:17.825 --> 00:18:19.244
What kind of a show are you guys

00:18:19.244 --> 00:18:20.681
putting on here today?

00:18:20.681 --> 00:18:22.843
I mean, the only class in this act

00:18:22.843 --> 00:18:25.290
is sitting next to me,
and I'm here to tell you

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:27.459
this boy's soul is intact.

00:18:27.459 --> 00:18:29.705
It's non-negotiable.

00:18:29.705 --> 00:18:30.785
You know how I know?

00:18:30.785 --> 00:18:34.172
Someone here, and I'm not going
to say who, offered to buy it.

00:18:34.172 --> 00:18:37.373
Only, Charlie here wasn't sellin'.

00:18:37.373 --> 00:18:38.439
- Sir, you're out of order.

00:18:38.439 --> 00:18:39.272
(slams gavel)

00:18:39.272 --> 00:18:41.082
- Out of order, I show you out of order.

00:18:41.082 --> 00:18:43.994
You don't know what out
of order is, Mr. Trask.

00:18:43.994 --> 00:18:45.579
I'd show you, but I'm too old.

00:18:45.579 --> 00:18:48.065
I'm too tired, I'm too blind.

00:18:48.065 --> 00:18:51.099
If I were the man I was five years ago,

00:18:51.099 --> 00:18:54.266
I'd take a flamethrower to this place.

00:18:56.670 --> 00:19:01.507
Out of order, who the hell
you think you're talking to?

00:19:01.507 --> 00:19:04.274
I've been around, you know?

00:19:04.274 --> 00:19:08.024
There was a time I could
see, and I have seen

00:19:09.986 --> 00:19:12.156
boys like these, younger than these,

00:19:12.156 --> 00:19:15.819
their arms torn out,
their legs ripped off.

00:19:15.819 --> 00:19:18.443
But there is nothing like a sight

00:19:18.443 --> 00:19:20.360
of an amputated spirit.

00:19:21.386 --> 00:19:24.053
There is no prosthetic for that.

00:19:25.886 --> 00:19:27.254
You think you're merely sending

00:19:27.254 --> 00:19:30.352
this splendid foot soldier
back home to Oregon

00:19:30.352 --> 00:19:32.462
with his tail between his legs,

00:19:32.462 --> 00:19:35.545
but I say you are executing his soul!

00:19:37.321 --> 00:19:38.154
And why?

00:19:39.722 --> 00:19:42.290
Because he's not a Baird man.

00:19:42.290 --> 00:19:43.123
Baird men.

00:19:44.955 --> 00:19:47.761
You hurt this boy, you're
going to be Baird bums.

00:19:47.761 --> 00:19:49.214
The lot of you.

00:19:49.214 --> 00:19:53.381
And Harry, Jimmy, Trent,
wherever you are out there.

00:19:54.962 --> 00:19:55.795
You too.

00:19:57.383 --> 00:19:59.608
(gavel pounding)

00:19:59.608 --> 00:20:01.162
- Stand down, Mr. Slade.

00:20:01.162 --> 00:20:03.422
- I'm not finished.

00:20:03.422 --> 00:20:07.589
As I came in here, I heard those
words cradle of leadership.

00:20:09.878 --> 00:20:14.045
Well, when the bough breaks,
the cradle will fall.

00:20:15.059 --> 00:20:16.705
And it has fallen here.

00:20:16.705 --> 00:20:18.237
It has fallen.

00:20:18.237 --> 00:20:21.433
Makers of men, creators of leaders,

00:20:21.433 --> 00:20:25.011
be careful what kind of
leaders you're producing here.

00:20:25.011 --> 00:20:28.834
I don't know if Charlie's
silence here today

00:20:28.834 --> 00:20:31.211
is right or wrong, I'm
not a judge or jury,

00:20:31.211 --> 00:20:33.855
but I can tell you this he won't

00:20:33.855 --> 00:20:36.772
sell anybody out to buy his future.

00:20:37.670 --> 00:20:41.170
And that, my friends, is called integrity.

00:20:42.075 --> 00:20:44.116
That's called courage.

00:20:44.116 --> 00:20:48.033
Now that's the stuff
leaders should be made of.

00:20:53.467 --> 00:20:57.975
Now, I have come to the
crossroads in my life,

00:20:57.975 --> 00:21:01.223
I always knew what the right path was

00:21:01.223 --> 00:21:03.390
without exception, I knew.

00:21:04.862 --> 00:21:06.360
But, I never took it.

00:21:06.360 --> 00:21:07.632
You know why?

00:21:07.632 --> 00:21:09.382
It was too damn hard.

00:21:13.195 --> 00:21:16.178
Now, here's Charlie, he's
come to the crossroads,

00:21:16.178 --> 00:21:20.054
he has chosen a path, it's the right path,

00:21:20.054 --> 00:21:23.138
it's a path made of principle.

00:21:23.138 --> 00:21:25.138
That leads to character.

00:21:27.330 --> 00:21:30.157
Let him continue on his journey.

00:21:30.157 --> 00:21:34.324
You hold this boy's future
in your hands, committee.

00:21:35.724 --> 00:21:38.641
It's a valuable future, believe me.

00:21:41.532 --> 00:21:42.708
Don't destroy it.

00:21:42.708 --> 00:21:43.625
Protect it.

00:21:44.624 --> 00:21:45.541
Embrace it.

00:21:47.976 --> 00:21:52.059
It's going to make you proud
one day, I promise you.

00:22:05.116 --> 00:22:08.576
I don't know what to say, really.

00:22:08.576 --> 00:22:11.493
Three minutes to the biggest battle

00:22:13.453 --> 00:22:15.620
of our professional lives.

00:22:16.811 --> 00:22:18.811
All comes down to today.

00:22:20.041 --> 00:22:24.208
In here, we heal as a team
or we're going to crumble.

00:22:28.580 --> 00:22:33.105
Inch by inch, play by
play til we're finished.

00:22:33.105 --> 00:22:35.496
We're in hell right now, gentleman.

00:22:35.496 --> 00:22:36.413
Believe me.

00:22:37.990 --> 00:22:41.907
And, we can stay here
get the kicked out of us,

00:22:43.502 --> 00:22:47.169
or we can fight our way
back into the light.

00:22:49.658 --> 00:22:51.658
We can climb out of hell.

00:22:53.184 --> 00:22:54.767
One inch at a time.

00:22:57.744 --> 00:23:00.490
Now, I can't do it for you.

00:23:00.490 --> 00:23:01.490
I'm too old.

00:23:04.213 --> 00:23:06.803
I look around, I see these young faces

00:23:06.803 --> 00:23:10.636
and I think, I mean, I
made every wrong choice

00:23:12.087 --> 00:23:14.337
a middle-aged man can make.

00:23:15.456 --> 00:23:19.289
I pissed away all my
money, believe it or not.

00:23:22.981 --> 00:23:26.314
I chased off anyone who's ever loved me.

00:23:28.055 --> 00:23:31.428
And lately, I can't even stand the

00:23:31.428 --> 00:23:33.511
face I see in the mirror.

00:23:36.156 --> 00:23:39.289
You know, when you get old in life

00:23:39.289 --> 00:23:41.977
things get taken from you.

00:23:41.977 --> 00:23:44.688
I mean, that's part of life.

00:23:44.688 --> 00:23:48.825
But, you only learn that
when you start losing stuff.

00:23:48.825 --> 00:23:52.992
You find out life's a game
in inches, so is football.

00:23:56.613 --> 00:24:00.618
Because in either game, life or football,

00:24:00.618 --> 00:24:02.718
the margin for error is so small.

00:24:02.718 --> 00:24:07.123
I mean, one half a step
too late or too early

00:24:07.123 --> 00:24:10.540
and you don't quite make it,
one half second too slow,

00:24:10.540 --> 00:24:12.522
too fast you don't quite catch it.

00:24:12.522 --> 00:24:16.782
The inches we need are
everywhere around us,

00:24:16.782 --> 00:24:19.374
they're in every break of the game,

00:24:19.374 --> 00:24:21.624
every minute, every second.

00:24:22.964 --> 00:24:25.552
(team cheers)

00:24:25.552 --> 00:24:28.135
On this team we fight for that inch.

00:24:28.135 --> 00:24:30.718
On this team, we tear ourselves

00:24:31.890 --> 00:24:36.007
and everyone else around
us, to pieces for that inch.

00:24:36.007 --> 00:24:39.257
We claw with our fingernails that inch,

00:24:40.267 --> 00:24:44.184
because we know when we
add up all those inches

00:24:45.966 --> 00:24:47.974
that's going to make the difference

00:24:47.974 --> 00:24:49.940
between winning and losing.

00:24:49.940 --> 00:24:51.967
(team cheers)

00:24:51.967 --> 00:24:54.050
Between living and dying.

00:24:56.251 --> 00:24:58.414
I'll tell you this, in any fight,

00:24:58.414 --> 00:25:02.581
it's the guy who's willing to
die who's going to win that inch.

00:25:04.526 --> 00:25:07.171
And I know if I'm going
to have any life anymore,

00:25:07.171 --> 00:25:09.224
it's because I'm still willing

00:25:09.224 --> 00:25:11.910
to fight and die for that inch.

00:25:11.910 --> 00:25:13.963
(team cheers)

00:25:13.963 --> 00:25:16.134
Because that's what living is.

00:25:16.134 --> 00:25:19.217
The six inches in front of your face.

00:25:20.442 --> 00:25:22.925
Now, I can't make you do it.

00:25:22.925 --> 00:25:26.574
You got to look at the guy next
to you, look into his eyes.

00:25:26.574 --> 00:25:29.096
Now, I think you're going to see a guy

00:25:29.096 --> 00:25:31.638
who will go that inch with you.

00:25:31.638 --> 00:25:35.830
You're going to see a guy
who will sacrifice himself

00:25:35.830 --> 00:25:37.866
for this team, because he knows

00:25:37.866 --> 00:25:42.033
when it comes down to it, you're
going to do the same for him.

00:25:42.979 --> 00:25:45.062
That's a team, gentleman.

00:25:46.497 --> 00:25:48.997
Either we heal now, as a team,

00:25:51.318 --> 00:25:53.818
or we will die as individuals.

00:26:00.220 --> 00:26:02.053
That's football, guys.

00:26:05.409 --> 00:26:06.826
That's all it is.

00:26:08.439 --> 00:26:10.689
Now, what are you going to do?

00:26:12.837 --> 00:26:15.337
(team cheers)

00:26:32.383 --> 00:26:33.604
- Thank you guys so much for watching,

00:26:33.604 --> 00:26:36.068
I made this video because
Dorian Wright asked me to.

00:26:36.068 --> 00:26:37.351
So, if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:26:37.351 --> 00:26:38.886
that you want me to profile next,

00:26:38.886 --> 00:26:41.307
leave it in the comments below
and I'll see what I can do.

00:26:41.307 --> 00:26:42.750
I'd also love to know which of Al's

00:26:42.750 --> 00:26:44.707
top 10 rules had the biggest
impact on you, and why.

00:26:44.707 --> 00:26:47.054
Leave it in the comments, and
I will join in the discussion.

00:26:47.054 --> 00:26:48.639
Finally, I want to give a quick shout out

00:26:48.639 --> 00:26:52.266
to Jason J-Ryze Fonceca
from ryze online dot com.

00:26:52.266 --> 00:26:54.582
Jason bought another copy of the book

00:26:54.582 --> 00:26:56.333
so I'm giving a quick shout out.

00:26:56.333 --> 00:26:58.397
Thank you so much, Jason,
it means a lot to me.

00:26:58.397 --> 00:26:59.230
For those of you guys watching,

00:26:59.230 --> 00:27:00.890
if you want your chance at a shout out

00:27:00.890 --> 00:27:03.697
in a future video, make sure
to buy a copy of the book,

00:27:03.697 --> 00:27:05.907
and email in your receipt
so we can keep track.

00:27:05.907 --> 00:27:07.310
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:27:07.310 --> 00:27:09.844
Continue to believe, or
whatever your one word is.

00:27:09.844 --> 00:27:11.677
And I'll see you soon.

00:27:17.638 --> 00:27:18.471
- Attica!

00:27:18.471 --> 00:27:19.304
Attica!

00:27:21.066 --> 00:27:22.530
I had this ability to mimic.

00:27:22.530 --> 00:27:25.048
I would mimic the movies, I would mimic

00:27:25.048 --> 00:27:27.713
any of the current singers, whether

00:27:27.713 --> 00:27:30.560
it was Al Jolson, or
Sinatra, whoever it was.

00:27:30.560 --> 00:27:33.882
And I then learned how to mimic

00:27:33.882 --> 00:27:36.664
situations and people
just for the fun of it.

00:27:36.664 --> 00:27:37.674
Where am I going to go?

00:27:37.674 --> 00:27:40.060
Where am I going to go?

00:27:40.060 --> 00:27:41.871
A lot of us who are actors have that

00:27:41.871 --> 00:27:43.244
sort of performer in us.

00:27:43.244 --> 00:27:47.014
Cause she got a great ass, and you got

00:27:47.014 --> 00:27:49.950
your head all the way up it.

00:27:49.950 --> 00:27:51.258
Robin Williams would be the example

00:27:51.258 --> 00:27:53.765
of someone who really lets you see it.

00:27:53.765 --> 00:27:55.158
You know, most of us have it,

00:27:55.158 --> 00:27:58.023
but we don't have his gifts
to be able to express it.

00:27:58.023 --> 00:27:59.599
But the needs are there.

00:27:59.599 --> 00:28:02.969
If you're a shy person, sometimes you hide

00:28:02.969 --> 00:28:07.288
behind an image that you
think somebody has of you.

00:28:07.288 --> 00:28:09.115
Say hello to my little friend!

00:28:09.115 --> 00:28:11.082
(explosion)

00:28:11.082 --> 00:28:13.650
It's almost a pose that you
can let your guard down,

00:28:13.650 --> 00:28:15.691
or commit to anything.

00:28:15.691 --> 00:28:17.319
And it's a way you can get into

00:28:17.319 --> 00:28:19.494
the habit of doing that, I did for awhile.

00:28:19.494 --> 00:28:20.524
- [Woman] Is it true?

00:28:20.524 --> 00:28:23.274
- Don't ask me about my business.

00:28:23.274 --> 00:28:26.730
Because, in a way, once
you start getting parts

00:28:26.730 --> 00:28:28.578
your learning process is going to get

00:28:28.578 --> 00:28:30.414
less and less anyway, cause you're

00:28:30.414 --> 00:28:32.443
going to be given roles that
you are so-called right for,

00:28:32.443 --> 00:28:35.202
quote unquote, which is type casting

00:28:35.202 --> 00:28:37.039
which is a lot of bologna, and you're

00:28:37.039 --> 00:28:38.398
going to get into that kind of thing.

00:28:38.398 --> 00:28:39.775
And you're not going to get a chance to

00:28:39.775 --> 00:28:41.430
grow and stretch yourself.

00:28:41.430 --> 00:28:43.640
(gunshots)

00:28:43.640 --> 00:28:44.623
Oh, nothing!

00:28:44.623 --> 00:28:48.540
I used to use auditions
as a way of practicing.

00:28:49.524 --> 00:28:50.741
What are you going to do about it?

00:28:50.741 --> 00:28:51.968
Usually they'd have a script they

00:28:51.968 --> 00:28:53.673
wanted me to read, I'd say oh I'll tell ya

00:28:53.673 --> 00:28:55.303
I've prepared a little
something, you know?

00:28:55.303 --> 00:28:59.021
What are you going to
do about it, asshole?

00:28:59.021 --> 00:29:00.525
And it was an opportunity to do something

00:29:00.525 --> 00:29:02.443
that I'd been working on, and you would

00:29:02.443 --> 00:29:05.204
work it out without ever
thinking about getting the role.

00:29:05.204 --> 00:29:07.267
I'm a journalist, think.

00:29:07.267 --> 00:29:08.265
Use your head.

00:29:08.265 --> 00:29:09.826
How do I operate as a journalist?

00:29:09.826 --> 00:29:11.430
By screwing the people who could

00:29:11.430 --> 00:29:13.054
provide me with information before

00:29:13.054 --> 00:29:14.939
they provided me with it.

00:29:14.939 --> 00:29:16.280
I used to dream about John a lot.

00:29:16.280 --> 00:29:19.005
Of course, when it first happened.

00:29:19.005 --> 00:29:22.088
And he was always alive in the dream.

00:29:39.711 --> 00:29:42.461
See, it's always there, you know?

00:29:43.409 --> 00:29:44.242
30 years.

00:29:51.481 --> 00:29:55.513
I was doing a play called
Basic Training of Pavlo Hummel

00:29:55.513 --> 00:29:59.096
on Broadway, and it
was a really great role

00:30:01.501 --> 00:30:04.368
and I had done things with it, and I

00:30:04.368 --> 00:30:07.815
had gotten the Tony Award,
and I was really, you know.

00:30:07.815 --> 00:30:10.812
Elmer John was coming to see it.

00:30:10.812 --> 00:30:13.753
And I don't like to know
when anyone's in the house,

00:30:13.753 --> 00:30:17.086
but I knew John was in the house, right?

00:30:18.821 --> 00:30:22.236
And every single thing
I did, every scene I did

00:30:22.236 --> 00:30:24.552
I was trying to impress John.

00:30:24.552 --> 00:30:27.922
And I knew I'm doing
this, I'm saying to myself

00:30:27.922 --> 00:30:30.456
I'm not doing this, I'm
trying to impress John.

00:30:30.456 --> 00:30:31.741
You know?

00:30:31.741 --> 00:30:35.617
And, it was over and I was really unhappy

00:30:35.617 --> 00:30:37.561
cause I knew I hadn't done.

00:30:37.561 --> 00:30:40.248
And John came back, and he said

00:30:40.248 --> 00:30:44.413
it was very impressive, Al (laughs).

00:30:44.413 --> 00:30:45.746
Very impressive.

00:30:47.124 --> 00:30:48.526
I went yes, John.

00:30:48.526 --> 00:30:49.656
I said, you know what.

00:30:49.656 --> 00:30:52.401
He said, he was so graceful though,

00:30:52.401 --> 00:30:54.143
he was so gracious about it all,

00:30:54.143 --> 00:30:56.807
but I said, you know,
I knew you were there

00:30:56.807 --> 00:30:58.561
and I was trying to, I've
been doing everything

00:30:58.561 --> 00:31:02.086
twice as much as I had to do it, you know?

00:31:02.086 --> 00:31:04.075
He says, it was good Al, it was good.

00:31:04.075 --> 00:31:05.886
It was good.

00:31:05.886 --> 00:31:07.314
I said you don't know, you don't

00:31:07.314 --> 00:31:08.973
realize that, you know,
you've been doing it.

00:31:08.973 --> 00:31:10.292
But I knew I had.

00:31:10.292 --> 00:31:12.912
So I was very, you know, he was like

00:31:12.912 --> 00:31:15.438
one of my idols so that
when he was coming to see me

00:31:15.438 --> 00:31:16.271
it was.

00:31:18.163 --> 00:31:20.514
You give all out, and
that's the worst thing

00:31:20.514 --> 00:31:24.450
you can do is try to impress
your friends who you love.

00:31:24.450 --> 00:31:25.677
- What's the best direction you've

00:31:25.677 --> 00:31:27.656
ever received on a set?

00:31:27.656 --> 00:31:31.121
- The best direction, well
one of the best things

00:31:31.121 --> 00:31:34.653
that comes to my mind now is this AD

00:31:34.653 --> 00:31:37.403
Burt Harris on Dog Day Afternoon.

00:31:39.297 --> 00:31:41.325
Well, he came up to me.

00:31:41.325 --> 00:31:44.156
I was in the, first of all he
kept me sober every morning.

00:31:44.156 --> 00:31:47.073
He hit me with those tomato juices.

00:31:49.438 --> 00:31:50.438
I'd come in.

00:31:51.514 --> 00:31:53.218
Some girl the other night was describing

00:31:53.218 --> 00:31:54.910
knowing me in Godfather II, how I

00:31:54.910 --> 00:31:57.431
came up with that crazy
hat and scarf, and stuff.

00:31:57.431 --> 00:32:00.192
And then I would have
to turn into Michael.

00:32:00.192 --> 00:32:02.590
Slowly it would just (groans).

00:32:02.590 --> 00:32:03.659
(laughter)

00:32:03.659 --> 00:32:05.748
And I'd become this.

00:32:05.748 --> 00:32:08.840
But anyway, Burt Harris came up to me.

00:32:08.840 --> 00:32:11.096
Smart kid, I mean, smart man.

00:32:11.096 --> 00:32:12.731
And I was in the middle of stuff

00:32:12.731 --> 00:32:14.483
and he just came over
and whispered in my ear,

00:32:14.483 --> 00:32:16.595
which we were in the
dark, they were out there

00:32:16.595 --> 00:32:19.664
with all the, I recommend the movie

00:32:19.664 --> 00:32:21.823
on a big screen if you could ever see it

00:32:21.823 --> 00:32:24.373
to see what Lumet does with a crowd

00:32:24.373 --> 00:32:26.551
and with the street, and to put you in it

00:32:26.551 --> 00:32:29.301
in such a way it just is kinetic.

00:32:30.326 --> 00:32:32.040
You can feel the energy around.

00:32:32.040 --> 00:32:35.714
It was a turning point in
films, and the way he did it.

00:32:35.714 --> 00:32:37.789
But anyway, this guy Burt, Burt Harris

00:32:37.789 --> 00:32:41.498
comes up to me, this is what
I love about movies, too.

00:32:41.498 --> 00:32:43.831
And he leans in and he says,

00:32:46.465 --> 00:32:47.382
say Attica.

00:32:52.266 --> 00:32:53.099
I said, what?

00:32:53.099 --> 00:32:56.714
He said go over the the crowd
and just yell out Attica.

00:32:56.714 --> 00:32:58.547
Okay, remember Attica.

00:33:00.006 --> 00:33:02.390
Because Attica was in prison and had had

00:33:02.390 --> 00:33:04.954
a lot of trouble, and
terrible prisons were killed.

00:33:04.954 --> 00:33:08.916
It was a real scandal,
it was so in the world.

00:33:08.916 --> 00:33:10.788
You know, it was right there.

00:33:10.788 --> 00:33:12.455
And I said, oh okay.

00:33:13.789 --> 00:33:15.673
He said go ahead, you just go out there.

00:33:15.673 --> 00:33:17.761
So I went out, and there I am,

00:33:17.761 --> 00:33:20.784
and there's all these
extras out there standing

00:33:20.784 --> 00:33:22.706
there all day long, and they start

00:33:22.706 --> 00:33:25.501
looking and the cops, you know.

00:33:25.501 --> 00:33:28.226
And I just say you
know, what about Attica.

00:33:28.226 --> 00:33:30.446
At one point I just add
to the cup, I says Attica.

00:33:30.446 --> 00:33:31.990
And I looked at the crowd, and I said

00:33:31.990 --> 00:33:33.897
hey, remember Attica!

00:33:33.897 --> 00:33:34.751
Huh?

00:33:34.751 --> 00:33:35.761
Attica!

00:33:35.761 --> 00:33:37.058
And they went yeah, Attica.

00:33:37.058 --> 00:33:38.147
Somebody says Attica.

00:33:38.147 --> 00:33:40.585
All of a sudden they say Attica!

00:33:40.585 --> 00:33:41.795
Attica!

00:33:41.795 --> 00:33:45.712
And this become an iconic
moment in the movies,

00:33:47.246 --> 00:33:50.006
and that's how it came about.

00:33:50.006 --> 00:33:50.839
- [Host] That's amazing.

00:33:50.839 --> 00:33:51.837
- Isn't that something?

00:33:51.837 --> 00:33:54.577
Yeah, I mean this is the way
these things happen in films.

00:33:54.577 --> 00:33:56.476
If you're open to it, and that's

00:33:56.476 --> 00:33:59.039
the kind of brain that Lumet had.

00:33:59.039 --> 00:34:01.346
And we would do things like that.

00:34:01.346 --> 00:34:05.473
And, you know, it just became this magic.

00:34:05.473 --> 00:34:08.147
When you're out there, you got the room,

00:34:08.147 --> 00:34:09.731
but when you're doing a movie

00:34:09.731 --> 00:34:11.270
in six weeks, they don't
have time for Attica.

